  "worked_solution": "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \u27e86{t}^{2},\\,\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{t}\u27e9\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\u27e8\\int 6{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e82{t}^{3}+{C}_{1},\\,\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}+{C}_{2}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=\u27e8-1,2\u27e9.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\u27e82(0)^{3}+{C}_{1},\\,\\sqrt{(0)^{3}}+{C}_{2}\u27e9 \\\\ \u27e8-1,2\u27e9 & =\u27e8{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have ${C}_{1}=-1$ and ${C}_{2}=2.$ Therefore, the position vector at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\u27e82{t}^{3}-1,\\,\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}+2\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the position vector at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =\u27e82(1)^{3}-1,\\,\\sqrt{(1)^{3}}+2\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e82-1,\\,1+2\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e81,\\,3\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
